#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447} :activity/id #uuid "cb0bf575-ae5d-5289-83a0-5fe4af2c7572" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862 :topic/id #uuid "c7162eba-3656-5b62-8b28-391c01075d02" :topic/math-academy-id 1086} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097910 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." :tutorial/id #uuid "10c42cfd-895e-5844-b943-efc5be835821" :tutorial/math-academy-id 759 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbe8a28f-ae9c-5fce-a185-e8029413c272" :step/math-academy-id 631552 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536862}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "9c8a3595-093e-5ea9-b728-e20ce01d63e5" :step/math-academy-id 631554 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097911 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ef3b0cc-ce87-5a51-9a34-0e45b0a342d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 761 :tutorial/title "General Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b767cb65-6bfb-54c1-b5e9-034fb71e87da" :step/math-academy-id 631553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551357}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1ed0244b-1489-5e87-9ec9-dd2f6566ad85" :step/math-academy-id 631557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551235}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "dfbce95d-2f31-5252-bde7-c60a98b5fb17" :step/math-academy-id 631560 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097914 :tutorial/content "There's nothing special about using a left Riemann sum to define a definite integral. A definite integral can also be defined as the limit of a right Riemann sum,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nNothing has changed, except that the index starts from $k=1$ instead of $k=0$, and goes up to $k=n.$ \n\n**Note:** In the limit as $n \\to \\infty,$ the left and right Riemann sums give *the exact same result*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1ade2ee-0ed4-5dad-b2fc-a3a517dd3f1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 764 :tutorial/title "Using a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1cf484cc-b168-5f89-a236-0169328a1312" :step/math-academy-id 631563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536861}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "0e12f8ef-911e-5e4b-85e0-2c32f12ef0b6" :step/math-academy-id 631564}] :activity/title "Defining Definite Integrals Using Left and Right Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096875}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551464} :activity/id #uuid "8c741e1d-cd57-5c83-a8c8-16d24858c4c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862 :topic/id #uuid "c7162eba-3656-5b62-8b28-391c01075d02" :topic/math-academy-id 1086} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f1f4b7-57c0-56e1-a117-8415a85c841e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550468} {:answer/id #uuid "34501985-19ea-50dc-8d60-3b788c0405c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550497} {:answer/id #uuid "51e949c9-0d0e-5fdb-bb95-ab16798be0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550562} {:answer/id #uuid "6591ea6e-2346-5c79-acf4-89b87782b7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550616} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa0ac59-d407-5702-ab18-aa0299efd7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550497} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc5e161-63d6-5b2f-8b4b-8f2692b551e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fea7dc0c-332b-5e65-b34c-aa74602e656a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113885" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+1)^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{2i}{n}+1)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "41bf41e2-b064-5bb6-9ccc-30e9675ed161" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ea339c-2b2b-5d4d-87b1-7c6bc85069c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550624} {:answer/id #uuid "726d3224-ba57-579d-ae29-183da135a7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=x$ and $b=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550655} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a12a2b-40b0-5e22-9231-966dc3b78cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550789} {:answer/id #uuid "ace7f773-c717-5546-8619-944725dddd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550834} {:answer/id #uuid "b72f4429-d885-5f60-880a-67e28d6f6c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=4x$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550789} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a33f6fd-b93c-5b4f-b219-979a3489cf29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0eabe319-fbfe-5f66-832c-db699322266a" :question/math-academy-id "q-114899" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "7a912a6d-06e4-5b08-99d4-ffcd3c9c8150"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce3626c4-68fc-5111-92bb-c6a72ef04b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{4ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393252} {:answer/id #uuid "51976397-23e1-57d8-a039-644bf6317f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(-2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393253} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2df163-f8cb-55a5-aa92-94fe12f6f8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393254} {:answer/id #uuid "680c2096-a3a6-56c7-af41-ad8e3a2a9b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{(2i/n)}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393255} {:answer/id #uuid "7e48b7d1-54ea-5e38-b291-212dbf407565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393254} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ae5ee-b93f-55d8-b530-ad34a29b014a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2f3eda7-3ae2-5c51-adcd-171934d8313e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73156" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{e}^{3e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{3e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=e,$ and the upper bound is $b=3e.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3e-e}{n}=\\frac{2e}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{3e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+i\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8a4c4ba2-931e-5de8-95fc-f866b5f90f9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9848c8b6-63aa-585c-9c3f-f09cf6a49bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386725} {:answer/id #uuid "4af5c4a2-1d06-5974-840c-84a0c9369afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386726} {:answer/id #uuid "0436ef1f-f984-5be7-bbed-df86ab8e327f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386727} {:answer/id #uuid "e02ec00a-498c-51f8-80b1-0c65fcdd05c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386728} {:answer/id #uuid "092157f2-38d5-50ad-be92-7e9d8c56698d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11b3b07-1feb-5e0e-9468-621e3b27f6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4385816f-7b9c-5f55-bc1f-96c9f8bc0f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d3e0a0e9-2ae7-5e40-acb0-bc04b722390d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a60d2e3-526f-5102-9725-20adf27ff09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=(x+5)^{2}$ and $b=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366153} {:answer/id #uuid "956bfc43-cbe9-5c70-afb3-2ec9e07e4855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $b=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366154} {:answer/id #uuid "0e292bf6-f233-544c-be90-3f443f1e8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $b=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366155} {:answer/id #uuid "e542d1e2-f6ee-58c6-9b96-6f23061dda78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=(x+7)^{2}$ and $b=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366156} {:answer/id #uuid "d569e473-f85e-560f-9a93-2537c1213925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=(x+5)^{2}$ and $b=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366155} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb235b7-5aa3-5617-a84a-c783d3c7a7ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6798d466-87fa-5086-ac4a-cf1eac1b223b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49141" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{5k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={x}_{k}^{2}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $b=7.$"} :step/id #uuid "ec476d83-723f-5784-843f-4fdb55768d1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551423}}] :activity/title "Defining Definite Integrals Using Left and Right Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550354}]}
